11. Security, privacy and customer trust
These requirements are not optional.
1. Separate each business's data through tenant isolation.
2. Customers can only access their own bookings.
3. Company employees can only access information allowed by their role.
4. Use server-side permission checks.
5. Do not expose private documents or internal notes.
6. Protect payment operations against duplicate submissions.
7. Validate all customer input on the server.
8. Use rate limits on public booking and verification endpoints.
9. Store only necessary personal information.
10. Define data retention and deletion procedures.
11. Record important booking and payment changes in an audit trail.
12. Use HTTPS and secure authentication.
13. Avoid predictable booking access links.
14. Make privacy and consent information understandable.
The storefront must also be usable on low-end Android devices and slower connections.

12. Edge cases the system must handle
A professional booking system is not defined only by the happy path.
Situation	Expected behavior
Vehicle becomes unavailable during checkout	Stop booking and offer alternatives
Customer clicks submit twice	Prevent duplicate bookings
Payment fails	Preserve booking state and offer safe retry
Customer closes browser	Allow secure recovery of booking
Company rejects request	Explain outcome and next steps
Driver becomes unavailable	Notify company and customer as appropriate
Customer changes dates	Recheck availability and recalculate
Flight is delayed	Allow staff to update pickup arrangements
Refund fails	Keep refund pending and alert staff
Storefront is disabled	Display a clear unavailable message
Business subscription expires	Apply defined access rules without losing booking records
Notification fails	Record failure and allow retry
Price changes after quote	Preserve the accepted quote or request customer approval for changes


