16. Recommended implementation order
Build in this sequence:
1. Inspect the existing KigaliFleet codebase and database.
2. Document what already works.
3. Identify missing backend capabilities.
4. Design tenant-aware storefront configuration.
5. Build the public homepage and business branding.
6. Connect published vehicles and services.
7. Build search and server-side availability.
8. Implement booking and pricing.
9. Implement booking status and dashboard synchronization.
10. Add confirmation and notifications.
11. Test security and edge cases.
12. Deploy a pilot with one real transport business.
Do not begin by redesigning every page. First establish the current implementation and preserve working functionality.

17. The business questions that must be answered before coding
There are product decisions that technical implementation cannot safely guess.
Question	Why it matters
Can a company enable instant booking?	Changes the booking workflow
Can a customer book without an account?	Determines authentication requirements
Can a booking contain multiple vehicles?	Changes data structure and pricing
Are deposits mandatory?	Changes checkout and payment
Can businesses set different pricing rules?	Changes pricing engine
Can a company accept manual payments?	Changes payment confirmation
Can a customer cancel online?	Changes booking lifecycle
Can customers request date changes?	Changes availability and pricing
Does every business need document verification?	Changes privacy and workflow
Can one business have multiple branches?	Changes business structure
Can one driver serve multiple bookings?	Changes assignment rules
What happens when a subscription expires?	Changes storefront availability and access

These questions should be resolved before finalizing the database and booking workflows.

18. Final product recommendation
The storefront should not be treated as a decorative website builder.

It is a revenue-generating interface connected to the fleet, booking, payment and customer-service operations of each company.

The most important user journey is:
Business shares storefront link

Customer explores vehicles and services

Customer checks availability and price

Customer submits booking

Company receives booking in KigaliFleet

Customer receives verified confirmation and next steps


My strongest recommendation: get this journey working reliably for one real business before adding advanced features. A beautiful storefront that accepts bookings it cannot fulfill is worse than a simple storefront that works.